}-{x}^{3}+9 \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}+3{x}^{2})i+({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-2y)j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =(2(2)(0){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}+3({2}^{2}))i+(({2}^{2}){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}-2(0))j \\\\  & =12\\,i+4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨12,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨12,4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{12} & =\\frac{y-0}{4} \\\\ 4(x-2) & =12y \\\\ x-2 & =3y \\\\ x-3y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423052 1062 #uuid "1470ea07-3b96-5965-8beb-c2e0041edd4a" 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423052 1063 #edb/ref 17592186398361 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423053 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423054 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423053 1062 #uuid "20ee70ca-88be-504d-b7aa-237d8d22e58a" 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423053 1063 #edb/ref 17592186373139 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423054 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423052 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423054 1062 #uuid "68e1c4ea-40a6-5a21-ae75-071da89109f6" 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423054 1063 #edb/ref 17592186423051 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423055 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423056 13194139534493 17592186045417 true] [17592186423055 